+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer resolution
+
+**Standard**: RFC 6901 (JSON Pointer) §3 (syntax), §4 (evaluation), §5 (JSON string representation, N/A: pointers are
+plain strings), §7 (error handling). §6 (URI fragment form) is out of scope. Documents are RFC 8259 JSON values.
+
+## User Story 1 - Resolve a pointer (P1)
+A service calls `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` and gets the referenced value.
+
+Acceptance (RFC 6901 §5 examples against its sample document):
+- `""` → whole document; `"/foo"` → `["bar","baz"]`; `"/foo/0"` → `"bar"`; `"/"` → 0; `"/a~1b"` → 1;
+  `"/c%d"` → 2; `"/e^f"` → 3; `"/g|h"` → 4; `"/i\\j"` → 5; `"/k\"l"` → 6; `"/ "` → 7; `"/m~0n"` → 8.
+- A referenced `null` is returned as `null` (not an error).
+
+## User Story 2 - Reject bad pointers and unresolvable references (P1)
+Throws an error when:
+- the pointer is not a string, or is non-empty and does not start with `/` (§3);
+- a `~` is not followed by `0` or `1` (§3);
+- a token does not exist: missing object member, array index out of range, `-` (§4: refers to the nonexistent
+  element after the last), array token that is not `0` or digits without a leading zero (§4), or traversal
+  into a primitive/`null` (§4).
+
+## Edge cases
+- Empty-string key is addressed by `"/"`; `~01` decodes to `~1` (decode `~1` first, then `~0`).
+- Only own members of objects are resolved: `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype`, `toString` etc. do not
+  resolve through the prototype chain (Constitution IV).
+- The document is never mutated; the returned value is the referenced value itself.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-1 `resolvePointer(document: unknown, pointer: string): unknown` exported from `src/index.ts`, documented.
+- FR-2 Failures throw a typed `JsonPointerError` (extends `Error`), also exported.
+- FR-3 No runtime dependencies.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Object-member tokens on arrays are errors; array indices are matched by the §4 `0|[1-9][0-9]*` rule only.
+- Duplicate-key and URI-fragment handling are out of scope.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- All RFC 6901 §5 examples pass; every error condition above is covered by a test; `npm test` and `tsc` are clean.
